Implement a Config class responsible for reading "Watch\watch-config.txt" as a simple key=value text file, ignoring comments and blank lines, and normalizing keys exactly as done in 0.4.0 so that existing field configurations remain valid without modification. The loader should open the configuration file using regular file access semantics and parse each line into a dictionary of strongly typed properties, including LogPath, window settings, dashboard port, and other tunables described in the PRD. When loading values, validate ranges and types, and for any invalid or out-of-range entry, log a clear message to the console and rewrite the configuration file to a corrected default value, preserving the PRD’s self-correcting behavior. In report mode, Config must not write back LogPath, whereas in dashboard mode, a Start action may update LogPath to reflect the last opened log as in 0.4.x. Implement precedence so that CLI arguments always override the configuration file when both are present, matching the existing semantics described in the PRD.
```csharp
public sealed class WatchConfig
{
    public string? LogPath { get; set; }
    public int DashboardPort { get; set; }
    // other keys...

    public static WatchConfig Load(string path)
    {
        var config = new WatchConfig();
        foreach (var line in File.ReadLines(path))
        {
            if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(line) || line.TrimStart().StartsWith("#"))
                continue;
            var parts = line.Split('=', 2);
            if (parts.Length != 2) continue;
            var key = parts[0].Trim();
            var value = parts[1].Trim();
            // parse and validate keys
        }
        // self-correction pass that rewrites invalid entries
        return config;
    }
}
```
Design Config so that it can be reloaded at runtime when the dashboard restart action is invoked, ensuring that changes in watch-config.txt are picked up without requiring a process restart. Align file path resolution with the documented PVSS_II.log location in Desigo CC projects, where logs live under the project log directory, and integrate knowledge of PVSS_II.log as the default scan file used by the Trace Viewer[3][8][10][11].

Introduce a Cli class that uses either System.CommandLine or a minimal custom parser to map command-line tokens into a strongly typed options object, preserving the existing switch names and semantics such as -LogPath, -OutPath, -Format, -Organize, -Severities, -Areas, -Driver, -TopN, -Entire, -LastHours, -LastMinutes, -From, -To, -Interactive, -NoPause, and -Report[18][19]. System.CommandLine’s Command and RootCommand abstractions can be leveraged to represent subcommands or modes, with the root command corresponding to the executable name and potential subcommands or options indicating whether the analyzer should run in dashboard or report mode[18][19]. Program.cs should initialize configuration, parse arguments, and then branch into either report-mode pipeline or dashboard-mode pipeline, with report mode exiting after finishing analysis and dashboard mode starting an HttpServer and tailing the log.
```csharp
var rootCommand = new RootCommand("Desigo PVSS log analyzer");
var reportOption = new Option<bool>("-Report", "Run in batch/report mode");
var logPathOption = new Option<string?>("-LogPath", "Override log path");
rootCommand.AddOption(reportOption);
rootCommand.AddOption(logPathOption);
rootCommand.SetHandler((bool report, string? logPath) =>
{
    var config = WatchConfig.Load("watch-config.txt");
    // apply CLI overrides
    if (report)
        RunReportMode(config);
    else
        RunDashboardMode(config);
}, reportOption, logPathOption);
return rootCommand.InvokeAsync(args).Result;
```
Implement interactive prompts for the -Interactive report mode, presenting questions similar to the 0.4.0 PowerShell host, with Enter-defaulted answers and an interactive manager pick list. For manager pick, fully support range syntax such as "1-3,10" by parsing comma-separated tokens, expanding ranges, and validating that selections map to actual managers. Ensure that argument parsing generates consistent help text and usage messages that are operator-friendly, and preserve existing short and long switch names without introducing breaking changes.

Create a LogParser class that opens the target PVSS_II.log file using FileAccess.Read and FileShare.ReadWrite so that the live Desigo CC or WinCC OA system can continue appending while the analyzer reads concurrently, matching the guidance that log viewing tools copy or open PVSS_II.log in read-only mode with sharing to avoid interfering with the project[3][8][10][11]. Implement an internal method that reads the file in buffered chunks, enumerating lines without loading the entire file into memory, and use ReadOnlySpan<char> over the buffer to avoid per-line substring allocations when parsing timestamps, severity levels, and modules[13][14][16]. By converting the input text to spans via .AsSpan, and slicing segments to extract tokens like date, severity string, and message, the parser can achieve near-zero allocation per line and substantial performance improvements versus the original PowerShell implementation dominated by per-line function calls[13][14][16].
```csharp
public IEnumerable<ReadOnlySpan<char>> EnumerateLines(string path)
{
    using var stream = new FileStream(path, FileMode.Open, FileAccess.Read, FileShare.ReadWrite);
    using var reader = new StreamReader(stream);
    string? line;
    while ((line = reader.ReadLine()) != null)
    {
        ReadOnlySpan<char> span = line.AsSpan();
        yield return span;
    }
}
```
Add support for analysis windows such as Entire, LastHours, LastMinutes, From, and To by computing timestamp boundaries and either skipping lines outside the window or adjusting the starting offset in the file. For dashboard mode, implement a catch-up phase that reads the historical window once, then transitions into a tailing loop that monitors for new bytes and processes appended lines, using file position and periodic polling. Tail logic should be resilient to log truncation or rotation, detecting file size shrink and re-seeking from the beginning when the PVSS_II.log file is reset by the system. The parser should surface parsed line objects or spans to the AnalysisState and RuleEngine components, keeping line parsing generic and reusable.

Design an AnalysisState class to hold aggregated data about the log, including counts per severity level, per module, per driver, pattern tables, lifecycle transitions, and any project-specific metrics used by 0.4.0’s Build-SnapshotObject. Implement a header parser that, for each ReadOnlySpan<char> line from LogParser, extracts the timestamp, severity string, module or manager name, and message text using span slicing and index operations rather than substring allocations, similar to real-world examples of log parsing with spans[13][14][16]. The parser should normalize severity values to the same categories recognized by existing tools, such as DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, and SEVERE, aligning with the filtering capabilities seen in related WinCC OA log viewers[1].
```csharp
public bool TryParseHeader(ReadOnlySpan<char> line, out LogEntry entry)
{
    // Example: "[2026-06-19 10:00:00] [ERROR] ManagerX: message"
    int firstClose = line.IndexOf(']');
    if (firstClose < 0) { entry = default; return false; }
    var timestampSpan = line.Slice(1, firstClose - 1);
    if (!DateTime.TryParse(timestampSpan, out var timestamp)) { entry = default; return false; }
    int secondOpen = line.Slice(firstClose + 1).IndexOf('[');
    int secondClose = line.Slice(firstClose + 1 + secondOpen + 1).IndexOf(']');
    var severitySpan = line.Slice(firstClose + 2 + secondOpen, secondClose);
    // further parsing for module and message
    entry = new LogEntry(timestamp, severitySpan.ToString(), /* module */, /* message */);
    return true;
}
```
Connect the parsed LogEntry objects to the AnalysisState by incrementing counters, updating pattern tables, and tracking lifecycle events such as project start, stop, and manager restarts that may be inferred from specific message signatures in PVSS_II.log[3]. Ensure that AnalysisState is structured to make it easy for SnapshotBuilder and RuleEngine to consume aggregated metrics and generate consistent output across report and dashboard APIs. Where complex lifecycle or driver-specific behavior existed as PowerShell helpers in 0.4.0, reimplement them as C# helper methods that operate on spans or pre-parsed entry objects to keep the hot path efficient.

Introduce a RuleDefinition record or class that encapsulates all attributes of a detection rule, including Id, Group, Label, Scope, pattern expressions, BucketBy criteria, Measure metrics, Sample configuration, and FindingAt thresholds, mirroring the structure in PvssRules.ps1 but in typed C# form. Implement a RuleEngine that consumes parsed LogEntry events and AnalysisState aggregates, applying each RuleDefinition’s pattern logic using compiled regular expressions or span-based matching to produce hits, buckets, measures, and findings in a unified pipeline. The engine should be data-driven, meaning that rules are expressed as rows in a table or as instances of RuleDefinition, not hard-coded if/else trees, thereby preserving the principle that adding a detection should remain “one row plus one test.” Configure compiled Regex instances up front for any pattern-based rules to reduce runtime overhead and reuse them across the process lifetime.
```csharp
public sealed record RuleDefinition(
    string Id,
    string Group,
    string Label,
    string Scope,
    Regex Pattern,
    Func<LogEntry, bool> BucketBy,
    Func<AnalysisState, double> Measure,
    Func<double, bool> FindingAt);

public sealed class RuleEngine
{
    private readonly IReadOnlyList<RuleDefinition> _rules;

    public RuleEngine(IReadOnlyList<RuleDefinition> rules)
    {
        _rules = rules;
    }

    public void Process(LogEntry entry, AnalysisState state)
    {
        foreach (var rule in _rules)
        {
            if (!rule.Pattern.IsMatch(entry.Message)) continue;
            // bucket, measure, and record findings
        }
    }
}
```
For the initial dev gate, restrict rules to single-scope semantics identical to 0.4.0, turning off any multi-scope or BACnet-specific variations that will be introduced in a later task. Port all existing rule definitions from PvssRules.ps1, including curated module cards and generic detections, making sure that scope filtering and ranking inputs behave exactly as previously. Where PowerShell contained hand-written analysis outside the rule table, such as BACnet flips, project lifecycle cycles, and Apogee orchestration families, implement equivalent C# logic as helper routines that either feed into the RuleEngine or supplement its outputs. Prepare fixtures and internal instrumentation to support parity checks at the snapshot level.

Create a SnapshotBuilder class that consumes AnalysisState and RuleEngine outputs and constructs a structured snapshot object representing the entire analysis, including sections for overview metrics, per-driver and per-module statistics, findings, detections, performance health, and any meta data such as tool version and generation timestamps. Then implement a ReportWriter that can render this snapshot into HTML, text, and JSON formats, honoring the -Format switch options Text, Html, and Both, and producing dashboard snapshot JSON in the same shape used by the existing UI. HTML output should follow the structure of the 0.4.0 reports, including headings, tables, and styling hooks expected by the dashboard, while text output should match plain-text formatting used in prior report modes. Use a tolerant comparison allowlist for meta fields such as meta.generated, tool/version banners, and perf.health URLs when running parity tests, but otherwise keep content byte-identical under the dev gate.
```csharp
