Integrate ReportWriter with the report-mode pipeline, ensuring that window selection, rule application, and snapshot building flow together to produce final outputs, and respect -OutPath and -NoPause semantics. Keep ReportWriter independent of HttpServer so that the same rendering logic can be reused for dashboard snapshots and report files. Document the dev gate process: run the C# pipeline with single-scope rules and compare HTML, text, and JSON snapshots against 0.4.0 outputs on a fixed corpus, only tolerating differences listed in an allowlist and failing on unexpected discrepancies.

Augment RuleDefinition and RuleEngine to allow Scope to be either a string or a list of scopes, enabling multi-scope rules that can operate across dual-driver scenarios per the PRD’s §2.1 upgrade. Implement BACnet-scoped rule families for the four Apogee-shaped drivers without widening existing Apogee scopes, ensuring that these new rules are clearly tagged and accounted for in detection counts and sections. Introduce configuration toggles or build-time constants that separate dev-gate behavior (single-scope only, no BACnet extras) from ship-gate behavior where multi-scope and BACnet rules are active; this separation allows parity validation before enabling enhancements. On the CLI side, ensure that interactive manager selection fully supports range syntax such as "1-3,10", parsing and validating ranges so that operators can easily select multiple managers for analysis as part of -Interactive prompts.
```csharp
public sealed record RuleDefinition(
    string Id,
    string Group,
    string Label,
    IReadOnlyList<string> Scopes,
    Regex Pattern,
    Func<LogEntry, bool> BucketBy,
    Func<AnalysisState, double> Measure,
    Func<double, bool> FindingAt);

public static IReadOnlyList<int> ParseManagerRange(string input)
{
    var result = new List<int>();
    foreach (var token in input.Split(','))
    {
        var trimmed = token.Trim();
        if (trimmed.Contains('-'))
        {
            var parts = trimmed.Split('-', 2);
            int start = int.Parse(parts[0]);
            int end = int.Parse(parts[1]);
            for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) result.Add(i);
        }
        else
        {
            result.Add(int.Parse(trimmed));
        }
    }
    return result.Distinct().OrderBy(i => i).ToList();
}
```
Update snapshot expectations and PROGRESS-V0.5 documentation to record known report deltas introduced by multi-scope and BACnet-scoped rules, and adjust ship-gate tests so that expected differences in detection counts and sections are treated as successes while unexpected diffs still fail. Maintain clear separation between Apogee and BACnet scopes to avoid conflating driver families. Ensure that manager range prompts integrate smoothly with the rest of the interactive CLI flow, presenting selected managers back to the operator for confirmation before analysis runs.

Develop an HttpServer class that uses HttpListener or a lightweight C# web framework to bind a local port on 127.0.0.1 and serve both static UI files from the "Watch\ui\" directory and JSON responses for the dashboard’s API routes. Configure the server to serve static files under a known root path, similar in concept to how frameworks such as Serverito map a static root URL to an on-disk path for serving assets, choosing an appropriate encoding for HTML, CSS, and JavaScript content[17]. Implement all /api/* routes inventoried in phase 5A, including endpoints such as /api/health, /api/pulse, /api/section, /api/manager, /api/snapshot, and control routes for start, pause, resume, and restart, backed by the SnapshotBuilder and AnalysisState so that data rendered in the dashboard matches 0.4.0’s structure.
```csharp
public sealed class HttpServer : IDisposable
{
    private readonly HttpListener _listener = new();

    public void Start(string prefix)
    {
        _listener.Prefixes.Add(prefix); // e.g. "http://127.0.0.1:5000/"
        _listener.Start();
        Task.Run(() => ListenLoop());
    }

    private async Task ListenLoop()
    {
        while (_listener.IsListening)
        {
            var context = await _listener.GetContextAsync();
            // route based on context.Request.Url
        }
    }
}
```
Static file serving should map URL paths to files under "ui\", returning 404 when files are missing and setting appropriate content-type headers and charset per file type. API routes should respond with JSON serialized snapshots and health data, using the same keys and types that the existing dashboard expects, including any Detections, modules, window descriptors, and perf metrics. Ensure that the server binds only to localhost and does not expose external network surfaces, satisfying the safety requirement that the analyzer bind 127.0.0.1 only in dashboard mode. Integrate HttpServer with Program and Cli so that dashboard mode starts the server, enters catch-up and tail loops, and shuts down cleanly on exit.

Extend the dashboard-mode pipeline so that, upon startup, the analyzer performs an Entire or selected window catch-up over PVSS_II.log, computing and exposing progress percentages based on file position and total size as the dashboard expects. After catch-up, transition into a tailing loop that continuously reads appended log lines, updates AnalysisState, RuleEngine, and snapshots, and periodically emits pulse updates via /api/pulse. Implement restart semantics such that, when the dashboard user triggers a restart, the analyzer re-reads watch-config.txt, applies any new configuration values, and reinitializes window selection and analysis state as appropriate while keeping HttpServer bound to localhost. Ensure that catch-up and tailing logic integrate seamlessly with HttpServer’s API routes so that the UI can reflect live progress, updated detections, and refreshed snapshots without requiring page reloads.
```csharp
public async Task RunDashboardAsync(WatchConfig config)
{
    using var parser = new LogParser(config.LogPath!);
    var state = new AnalysisState();
    var rules = RuleEngineFactory.Create(config);
    // catch-up phase
    await parser.CatchUpAsync(state, rules);
    // enter tail phase
    await parser.TailAsync(state, rules, cancellationToken);
}
```
Implement progress reporting by tracking bytes read versus total file length, and surface this information via /api/health or /api/pulse so that the dashboard can render catch-up percentages and convey when the analyzer has transitioned into live tailing. For restart behavior, gracefully cancel the current tail loop, reload configuration via Config.Load, update state or reset where necessary, and re-run catch-up plus tail; ensure that LogPath updates in the dashboard mode still write back to watch-config.txt as described in the PRD while report mode never modifies LogPath. Keep concurrency concerns in mind, coordinating log reading, state updates, and HTTP request handling so that snapshots reflect a consistent view of the analysis.

Using the same ~50 MB static PVSS_II.log corpus used for 0.4.0 timing notes, run the C# analyzer in report mode and dashboard mode, recording entire-file scan and HTML write times and comparing them to the original ~8-minute PowerShell baseline. Aim to document performance improvements in PROGRESS-V0.5.md, noting that aspirations such as under 5 seconds and stretch goals under 1 second on warm disks are informative but not hard release criteria. Employ profiling tools and instrumentation to identify any hotspots, focusing on line parsing, rule evaluation, and snapshot rendering, and leverage span-based parsing and compiled regexes to ensure that per-line allocations remain minimal[13][14][16]. Evaluate cold Entire parse for dashboard mode specifically, determining whether re-parse on window flips is noticeable; if cold Entire parse is already effectively instant (for example under around 0.5–1 second), skip implementing an Entire-window cache for 0.5.0 per the PRD’s guidance, otherwise plan and implement a cache late in the phase that clones analysis state after catch-up and reuses it when revisiting Entire, ensuring that cache saving does not block the host and that results are served before caching.
```csharp
public sealed class PerfMetrics
{
    public TimeSpan ReportEntireDuration { get; set; }
    public TimeSpan DashboardColdEntireDuration { get; set; }
}

public void MeasureAndDecideCache()
{
    var metrics = RunPerfMeasurements();
    if (metrics.DashboardColdEntireDuration > TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1))
    {
        ImplementEntireCache();
    }
}
```
In parallel, perform parity verification against 0.4.0 outputs under the dev gate by comparing snapshot HTML, text, and JSON outputs for static logs, allowing only documented meta differences and failing on unexpected content changes. After enabling §2.1 exceptions, adjust tests to expect known count and section differences for multi-scope and BACnet rules while still failing on unexpected diffs, and validate dashboard API contracts, config behavior, and launchers to ensure that all acceptance criteria are met. Record all findings and decisions in PROGRESS-V0.5.md, especially the rationale for implementing or skipping the Entire-window cache in 0.5.0.
